# Each defence in the gaze fix, removed on its own, with the test that noticed.
# 2026-09-10. Runner: a scratchpad script that swaps one or two strings, runs the
# four avatar test files, and then writes back the exact bytes it read at the
# top. It never runs `git checkout -- <path>`, because these files carried
# uncommitted work while this ran and a checkout would have taken it with them.
# It does not undo a mutation by reverse string swap either: W4's first part
# replaces a line with the empty string, and reversing that is `replace('', old)`,
# which python splices between every character. It did exactly that to the engine
# file on the first attempt, 1777 lines into 170k, which is why the restore is
# now "write `s` back" and the assertion after it compares the whole file.
#
# Scope for every row: avatarPitch.test.ts, avatarBow.test.ts,
# avatarGuideEngine.wiring.test.ts and avatarMode.test.ts, 150 tests together.
#
#   A1  aimPitchPose drops the facing from the HEAD half only
#   A2  aimPitchPose drops it from the NECK half only
#   A3  aimYawPose's three terms take a sign. On a 0.x body fwd is -1, so on
#       that family this is `fwd * yaw * ...` written out; on the two 1.0
#       bodies version-signing would be a no-op, so a plain flip is strictly
#       stronger.
#   W1  the engine computes the whole gaze inline again instead of calling
#       aimPitchPose and aimYawPose. Identical motion, so only the tests that
#       read the engine's source can see it, and it breaks two properties at
#       once, which is why it reddens two of them.
#   W2  the engine reads facingSign('1') instead of the body's own version
#   W3  the gaze call is kept but handed a literal 1 instead of the facing
#   W4  the facing is hoisted out of frame() to the top of initAvatarGuide, a
#       plausible "compute it once" follow-up. Every string the wiring test
#       matched before this mutation was written survives it, and `vrm` is null
#       up there, so `?? '1'` pins fwd to +1 for the life of the engine and
#       every 0.x body regresses. This is why those assertions are scoped to
#       frameBody() rather than to the whole file.
#   W5  the eye target becomes `Math.sin(pitch) * 4`. That expression is what
#       DEFINES which way `pitch` means, and avatarPitch.test.ts holds only a
#       copy of it, so before this assertion existed the flip left the bones and
#       the target disagreeing again with every other test green.
#   W6  the GESTURE call is handed a literal 1. Same shape as W3 one layer up,
#       and it exists because the two assertions in that test are two defences:
#       a reviewer pointed out that W2 and W4 both land on the first one.
#   M1  `tilt`, a version-blind gesture, starts writing the chest pitch
#       (`o.cx`) that no gesture in the table writes today
#
# WHICH TEST EACH ONE REDDENS. The map is not one-to-one and does not need to
# be: what matters is that no defence is unguarded and no mutation passes. W1
# reddens two because it removes two properties in one edit. W3 shares a test
# with W1, and W2, W4 and W6 share one with each other, each attacking it from a
# different side.
#
#   A1  head term agrees with the eye target when looking down
#       head term agrees with the eye target when looking up
#   A2  neck term agrees with the eye target when looking down
#       neck term agrees with the eye target when looking up
#   A3  all three yaw terms turn her toward her own left, on either version
#   W1  poses the gaze through avatarMode rather than a second copy of the ratios
#       resolves the gaze pitch against the body being posed, not a literal
#   W2  reads the facing off the body every frame, and gives it to the gestures
#   W3  resolves the gaze pitch against the body being posed, not a literal
#   W4  reads the facing off the body every frame, and gives it to the gestures
#   W5  sends the eye target DOWN for a positive pitch, which is what defines the sign
#   W6  reads the facing off the body every frame, and gives it to the gestures
#   M1  tilt
#
